(** The square root of 2 is irrational. This file says what irrational
    means; the theorem, [sqrt2_irrational : irrational (sqrt 2)], is in
    sqrt2_irrational.proof, proved top down: the theorem comes first, and
    its proof reduces it to simpler and simpler statements, each proved in
    turn. sqrt2_irrational_bottom_up.proof proves the same bottom up, as
    separate named lemmas.

    If the program builds, the theorem is proved: every step is checked,
    and nothing in it is assumed ([Axiom], [Admitted]) or left to run time
    ([Any]). Being about Stdlib's real numbers, it rests on the axioms those
    are built on (classical logic and functional extensionality);
    [Print Assumptions sqrt2_irrational] lists them. *)

(** A real number is irrational when no rational equals it. [Q2R] turns a
    rational into a real. *)
irrational (x : R) := ~ exists q : Q, Q2R q = x.
